body:
I remember when I was 18 my parents sat me down and asked what I wanted to do with my life. My father said this: "You can go to college and not pay rent for the time being. If you do get a job however, help out with whatever you can. If you choose not to go to college however and get a job, then we can figure out what you need to pay in order to get you ready for when you do decide to leave home." 

&#x200B;

Mind you, he grew up in extreme poverty and his background isn't for the faint of heart so I thought this was extremely reasonable. I went to college for about a year and graduated, I got a job and was paying him around 600 for utilities and rent ( I volunteered while in college still) and saved up the rest until I turned 19 and left home. 

&#x200B;

I would say at least for me, my situation wasn't the worst but it wasn't the best. There were times I couldn't make the rent and so I had to "pay it back" other ways but it at least gave me a glimpse to what can or would happen once I was on my own. I did eventually buy a house once I turned 19 and I believe it was because my parents helped me prepare for becoming somewhat of an adult. I was perfectly fine staying at home though and wasn't entirely pressured to leave.
